teps":["Keep **verb + object** together, then add the manner adverb.","The direct object follows the verb, and **carefully** follows the object.","Choose **They prepared the evening meal carefully.**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Carefulness is a noun and does not modify the action in this position.","B":"The direct object follows the verb, and **carefully** follows the object.","C":"The adverb separates the transitive verb from its object in an awkward neutral order.","D":"Careful is an adjective, not the adverb needed here."}}},{"id":"74bf6065-0c0c-5939-a0c1-f7937339a849","topicSlug":"adverbs-position","level":"A2","prompt":"Choose neutral word order for a routine involving the rowing boat.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"She repairs the rowing boat usuallys."},{"id":"B","text":"She usual repairs the rowing boat."},{"id":"C","text":"She usually repairs the rowing boat."},{"id":"D","text":"She repairs usually the rowing boat."}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Place **usually before the main lexical verb**, while keeping verb and object together.","explanation":"The frequency adverb **usually** normally precedes the lexical verb in neutral order.","keywords":["neutral-frequency"],"skill":"neutral-frequency","contextKey":"adverbs-position-50","fingerprint":"eae8716528acbb6cc720ab3e1f290d12ab1e4d1159beebea3bf37b8afd7be715","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-adverbials"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Place **usually before the main lexical verb**, while keeping verb and object together.","The frequency adverb **usually** normally precedes the lexical verb in neutral order.","Choose **She usually repairs the rowing boat.**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Usually is an adverb and does not take an -s agreement ending.","B":"Usual is an adjective; this position requires the adverb usually.","C":"The frequency adverb **usually** normally precedes the lexical verb in neutral order.","D":"This puts usually between the transitive verb and its object."}}},{"id":"1a5d1329-fddb-5ef7-a56f-65cd44539eab","topicSlug":"adverbs-position","level":"A2","prompt":"Use the adverb, not the adjective: “The assistant is ____ ready to repair the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"usual"},{"id":"B","text":"usuals"},{"id":"C","text":"usualness"},{"id":"D","text":"usually"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The blank modifies how often the assistant is ready, so it needs a **frequency adverb**.","explanation":"After be, **usually** gives the frequency of the state ready.","keywords":["frequency-after-be"],"skill":"frequency-after-be","contextKey":"adverbs-position-50","fingerprint":"15985d38f4befd2049e9488fd74fba3d5e6e7ca30013a6333d5b4802f6636ed1","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-adverbials"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The blank modifies how often the assistant is ready, so it needs a **frequency adverb**.","After be, **usually** gives the frequency of the state ready.","Choose **usually**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Usual is an adjective and cannot modify ready as a frequency adverb here.","B":"Usuals is not the frequency adverb.","C":"A noun naming a quality does not supply the needed frequency adverb.","D":"After be, **usually** gives the frequency of the state ready."}}},{"id":"bdd483c4-0854-52cf-a120-db7c4e669503","topicSlug":"adverbs-position","level":"A2","prompt":"Describe the action’s manner: “They repair the rowing boat ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"carefulness"},{"id":"B","text":"care"},{"id":"C","text":"carefully"},{"id":"D","text":"careful"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Ask **how?** about the action, rather than asking what the person is like.","explanation":"**Carefully** tells us how they perform the action.","keywords":["manner-adverb"],"skill":"manner-adverb","contextKey":"adverbs-position-50","fingerprint":"8333804f51a16872d24be69c17585e5502b0886c9efac1450cfaf13f15eab84b","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-adverbials"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Ask **how?** about the action, rather than asking what the person is like.","**Carefully** tells us how they perform the action.","Choose **carefully**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Carefulness is a noun, not a manner adverb.","B":"Care is not the required manner adverb.","C":"**Carefully** tells us how they perform the action.","D":"Careful is an adjective; the task asks for an adverb describing the action."}}},{"id":"a1ed3f12-b0f7-5273-ada0-42088a39854f","topicSlug":"adverbs-position","level":"A2","prompt":"Keep the object beside the verb in this neutral sentence about the rowing boat.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"They repaired the rowing boat carefully."},{"id":"B","text":"They repaired carefully the rowing boat."},{"id":"C","text":"They careful repaired the rowing boat."},{"id":"D","text":"They repaired the rowing boat carefulness."}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Keep **verb + object** together, then add the manner adverb.","explanation":"The direct object follows the verb, and **carefully** follows the object.","keywords":["manner-word-order"],"skill":"manner-word-order","contextKey":"adverbs-position-50","fingerprint":"d32643a33f2385378276c6b41db8613f97dbcc129379c3b4cacd27e65392eca0","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-adverbials"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Keep **verb + object** together, then add the manner adverb.","The direct object follows the verb, and **carefully** follows the object.","Choose **They repaired the rowing boat carefully.**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The direct object follows the verb, and **carefully** follows the object.","B":"The adverb separates the transitive verb from its object in an awkward neutral order.","C":"Careful is an adjective, not the adverb needed here.","D":"Carefulness is a noun and does not modify the action in this position."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Describe how often and how an action happens, while placing the adverb where its meaning is clear.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Frequency before the main verb","explanation":"Frequency adverbs such as usually, often and never normally go before an ordinary lexical verb. Their position may shift for focus, but neutral order gives a reliable starting point. Never is already negative in meaning.","pattern":"She **usually walks**. / They **never complain**.","commonMistakes":["Do not add not merely because never is negative: they never complain is already negative in meaning."],"examples":["Choose neutral word order for a routine involving the visitor guide. → **She usually prepares the visitor guide.**","Choose neutral word order for a routine involving the old portrait. → **She usually restores the old portrait.**"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"After be and after the first auxiliary","explanation":"With main-verb be, a frequency adverb usually follows be. With a multi-part verb phrase, it often follows the first auxiliary. Always can appear in other positions for emphasis; these are neutral-order exercises.","pattern":"She is **usually** early. / They have **often** visited.","commonMistakes":["She usually is early can be emphatic, so a neutral-order instruction is needed when comparing positions."],"examples":["Use the adverb, not the adjective: “The assistant is **usually** ready to prepare the visitor guide.”","Use the adverb, not the adjective: “The assistant is **usually** ready to restore the old portrait.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Manner and adjective forms","explanation":"A manner adverb describes how an action is done. Many are formed with -ly, but not all: fast and hard are common adverbs without -ly. An adjective usually describes a noun or follows a linking verb.","pattern":"They worked **carefully**. / The work was **careful**.","commonMistakes":["Hard means with effort; hardly means almost not. They are not ordinary form variants with the same meaning."],"examples":["Describe the action’s manner: “They prepare the visitor guide **carefully**.”","Describe the action’s manner: “They restore the old portrait **carefully**.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Keep the object beside its verb","explanation":"A short manner adverb often goes after the object, or before the main verb. Do not normally put it between a transitive verb and its direct object. Position also changes scope: only can focus on different parts of a sentence.","pattern":"She read **the letter carefully**.","commonMistakes":["She read carefully the letter is awkward neutral order; put carefully after the object or before read."],"examples":["Keep the object beside the verb in this neutral sentence about the visitor guide. → **They prepared the visitor guide carefully.**","Keep the object beside the verb in this neutral sentence about the old portrait. → **They restored the old portrait carefully.**"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["She **usually walks**. / They **never complain**.","She is **usually** early. / They have **often** visited.","They worked **carefully**. / The work was **careful**.","She read **the letter carefully**."]},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["comparatives-and-superlatives"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
